  "moduleId": "m00351",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00351:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Chain-Growth Polymers"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "7884bd87-8952-417b-85c8-3a318cefa02b"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Benzoyl peroxide reacts with heat to form benzoyloxy radical, which reacts to form phenyl radical. Phenyl radical reacts with ethene to form ethylbenzene radical; continued polymerization with ethene forms product."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "Acid reacts with a vinyl monomer bonded to an electron-donating group to form a cationic intermediate. Continued reaction with units of monomer result in polymerization (always via cation formation)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Isobutylene reacts with hydrogen trifluoro(hydroxy)borate to form polyisobutylene depicted inside parentheses with subscript n."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "A nucleophile reacts with a vinyl monomer bonded to an electron-withdrawing group to form an anionic intermediate. Continued reaction with units of monomer result in polymerization (always via anion formation)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "Butyl lithium reacts with styrene to form an anionic intermediate that further reacts with styrene. The process repeats many times to give polystyrene."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00351:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00351:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "Methyl alpha cyanoacrylate reacts with a nucleophile with lone pair to give an intermediate, that forms a polymer. The figure below shows the structure of dermabond, 2-ethylhexyl alpha cyanoacrylate."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_31_01_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
